/* AUTO-GENERATED by build.py from source/interview-questions/ - edit the source, not this file. */
LP.questionsBulk([{"q": "What is the difference between WHERE and HAVING?", "a": "**WHERE** filters individual rows *before* grouping/aggregation. **HAVING** filters *groups* *after* aggregation, so it can use aggregate functions.\n\n```sql\nSELECT city, COUNT(*) c\nFROM customers\nWHERE active = true       -- per-row filter first\nGROUP BY city\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 100;    -- per-group filter after\n```", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Explain the types of SQL JOINs.", "a": "- **INNER JOIN** — only rows that match in both tables.\n- **LEFT JOIN** — all left rows + matches from right (NULLs where none).\n- **RIGHT JOIN** — all right rows + matches from left.\n- **FULL OUTER JOIN** — all rows from both, matched where possible.\n- **CROSS JOIN** — every combination (Cartesian product).\n\nTip: to find rows in A with no match in B, use `LEFT JOIN ... WHERE b.id IS NULL`.", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "How do you find the second-highest salary in a table?", "a": "Cleanest with a window function:\n\n```sql\nSELECT DISTINCT salary FROM (\n  SELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk\n  FROM employees\n) t WHERE rnk = 2;\n```\n\nUse `DENSE_RANK` so tied top salaries don't skip the 2nd value. A simpler (but tie-fragile) version: `SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees);`", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What are window functions and when would you use them?", "a": "A window function computes across a set of rows *related to the current row* **without collapsing them** like GROUP BY. Pattern: `func() OVER (PARTITION BY ... ORDER BY ...)`.\n\nUse them for: running totals, moving averages, ranking (`ROW_NUMBER`, `RANK`), 'top-N per group', and period-over-period change (`LAG`/`LEAD`). Example — latest row per customer:\n```sql\nROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY cust_id ORDER BY ts DESC) = 1\n```", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "A query is slow. How do you optimise it?", "a": "1. **Run `EXPLAIN`/`EXPLAIN ANALYZE`** to find the real bottleneck (full scan vs index seek, bad join order).\n2. **Add indexes** on columns used in JOINs and WHERE filters.\n3. **Select only needed columns**; avoid `SELECT *`.\n4. **Filter early** to shrink rows before joins/aggregation.\n5. **Avoid functions on indexed columns** in WHERE (`date >= '2024-01-01'` not `YEAR(date)=2024`).\n6. **Partition** large tables (e.g. by date) so the engine prunes.\n7. Consider **materialised views** for repeated heavy aggregations.\n\nAlways start with EXPLAIN — optimise the measured bottleneck, not a guess.", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is a CTE and why use it over a subquery?", "a": "A **CTE** (Common Table Expression, `WITH name AS (...)`) names a temporary result set. Benefits over nested subqueries: **readability** (build complex logic step-by-step), **reusability** (reference it multiple times), and support for **recursion** (`WITH RECURSIVE` for hierarchies). Functionally similar to a subquery; mostly a clarity win.", "topic": "SQL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What's the difference between a list, a tuple, and a set in Python?", "a": "- **list** `[1,2,3]` — ordered, **mutable**, allows duplicates.\n- **tuple** `(1,2,3)` — ordered, **immutable** (can't change), allows duplicates; usable as dict keys.\n- **set** `{1,2,3}` — **unordered**, mutable, **no duplicates**; fast membership tests.\n\nUse a set to dedupe or check membership; a tuple for fixed records/keys; a list for ordered, changeable collections.", "topic": "Python", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is a list comprehension? Give an example.", "a": "A concise way to build a list by transforming/filtering an iterable.\n```python\nsquares = [x*x for x in range(10)]\nevens   = [x for x in nums if x % 2 == 0]\n```\nMore readable and usually faster than an equivalent `for` loop with `.append()`. There are also dict and set comprehensions: `{k: v for k, v in pairs}`.", "topic": "Python", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "How would you process a file too large to fit in memory?", "a": "Process it **in chunks/streaming** instead of loading it all:\n- Iterate line by line: `for line in open(file):` (lazy, one line at a time).\n- With pandas: `pd.read_csv(file, chunksize=100_000)` and process each chunk.\n- Use generators to avoid materialising big lists.\n- For truly big data, switch to a distributed engine (**Spark**) that partitions across machines.\n\nKey idea: keep memory bounded by never holding the whole dataset at once.", "topic": "Python", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Explain *args and **kwargs.", "a": "They let a function accept a variable number of arguments. `*args` collects extra **positional** args into a tuple; `**kwargs` collects extra **keyword** args into a dict.\n```python\ndef f(*args, **kwargs):\n    print(args)    # (1, 2)\n    print(kwargs)  # {'x': 10}\nf(1, 2, x=10)\n```\nUseful for wrappers/decorators and flexible APIs.", "topic": "Python", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is the GIL and how do you work around it for performance?", "a": "The **GIL (Global Interpreter Lock)** lets only one thread execute Python bytecode at a time, so CPU-bound multithreading doesn't speed up. Work-arounds:\n- **I/O-bound** work (API calls, disk): threads or `asyncio` still help, because the GIL is released during I/O.\n- **CPU-bound** work: use **multiprocessing** (separate processes, separate GILs), or push computation into C-backed libraries (**NumPy/pandas/PyArrow**) or distributed engines (**Spark**).\n", "topic": "Python", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "Explain transformations vs actions in Spark and lazy evaluation.", "a": "**Transformations** (`select`, `filter`, `join`, `groupBy`) are **lazy** — they build a plan (lineage) but don't execute. **Actions** (`show`, `count`, `collect`, `write`) **trigger** execution.\n\nLaziness lets Spark see the whole plan and optimise it (Catalyst): combining steps, reordering filters, minimising data movement. Nothing runs until an action is called.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is a shuffle and how do you reduce its cost?", "a": "A **shuffle** moves data across the network so matching keys land on the same executor — triggered by `groupBy`, `join`, `distinct`, repartition. It's expensive (network + disk + serialization). Reduce it by:\n- **Broadcast joins** for small tables: `df.join(broadcast(small), 'key')`.\n- **Filtering/selecting early** to move less data.\n- **Pre-partitioning/bucketing** on the join key.\n- **Handling skew** (salting hot keys, AQE skew join).\n- Tuning `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` to the data size.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "How do you handle data skew in Spark?", "a": "Skew = one key has far more rows than others, so one task does most of the work while others idle. Fixes:\n- **Salting**: add a random suffix to the hot key to split it across partitions, then aggregate in two stages.\n- **Broadcast** the smaller side to avoid the skewed shuffle join.\n- Enable **Adaptive Query Execution (AQE)** with skew-join handling (Spark 3+).\n- **Filter/isolate** the hot key and process it separately.\n- Repartition on a better-distributed key.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "RDD vs DataFrame vs Dataset — which do you use and why?", "a": "- **RDD**: low-level, untyped distributed objects; flexible but **no Catalyst optimisation**.\n- **DataFrame**: high-level, table-like, optimised by Catalyst & Tungsten — **use this 99% of the time**.\n- **Dataset**: typed DataFrame (Scala/Java mainly).\n\nDefault to DataFrames/Spark SQL for performance and simplicity; drop to RDDs only for rare low-level control.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is partitioning and why does it matter?", "a": "Spark splits data into **partitions** processed in parallel by executors. Good partitioning (~128MB each) keeps all cores busy; too few = idle executors and OOM risk, too many = scheduling overhead. On **write**, `partitionBy('date')` creates folders so future reads prune irrelevant data (partition pruning) — big speed/cost wins. It's the main lever for parallelism and I/O efficiency.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Explain SCD Type 1 vs Type 2.", "a": "**SCD** = how dimension attributes change over time.\n- **Type 1**: *overwrite* the value — simple, but **history is lost**. Use for corrections.\n- **Type 2**: *add a new row* for the new value, keeping the old. Uses a **surrogate key** per version plus `start_date`, `end_date`, `is_current`. **Full history preserved** — facts point to the version valid at the time. Implemented with a MERGE/upsert (or dbt snapshot). Type 2 is the default when you must track change.", "topic": "Data Modeling", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Star schema vs snowflake schema?", "a": "Both are dimensional models (facts + dimensions).\n- **Star**: dimensions are flat/denormalized. Fewer joins, simpler, faster — the **default**.\n- **Snowflake**: dimensions are normalized into sub-tables. Saves storage, more joins, more complexity. Use only when dimensions are very large or shared.\n\nFact tables hold measures + foreign keys; dimensions hold descriptive context you slice by.", "topic": "Data Modeling", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is normalization and what are the normal forms?", "a": "**Normalization** organises tables to remove redundancy so each fact is stored once (preventing update anomalies).\n- **1NF**: atomic values, no repeating groups.\n- **2NF**: 1NF + no partial dependency on part of a composite key.\n- **3NF**: 2NF + no transitive dependency (non-key → non-key).\n\nOLTP databases are normalized; analytical warehouses often **denormalize** (star schema) for fast reads.", "topic": "Data Modeling", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is the 'grain' of a fact table and why does it matter?", "a": "The **grain** is what a single row represents — e.g. 'one row per order line item' or 'one row per account per day'. Defining it first is the most important modelling decision: it determines which dimensions and measures fit, and **mixing grains in one table produces wrong aggregations**. State the grain explicitly before designing columns.", "topic": "Data Modeling", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Surrogate key vs natural key — which and why?", "a": "A **natural key** is a real-world identifier (email, SSN); a **surrogate key** is a system-generated meaningless id (1,2,3…). Warehouses prefer **surrogate keys** because they're stable when source systems change, decouple the warehouse from source quirks, and are essential for **SCD Type 2** (each historical version needs its own key). Keep the natural key as an attribute for lookups.", "topic": "Data Modeling", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "ETL vs ELT — what's the difference and when use each?", "a": "**ETL** transforms data *before* loading (on a separate engine) — traditional, for limited-compute warehouses. **ELT** loads raw first, then transforms *inside* the warehouse — modern, leveraging cheap, powerful cloud warehouses (Snowflake/BigQuery) and tools like dbt. ELT keeps raw data so you can reprocess if logic changes; it's the default for the modern data stack.", "topic": "ETL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What does idempotency mean for a pipeline and how do you achieve it?", "a": "**Idempotent** = running the pipeline multiple times yields the same result as running it once — critical because jobs fail and get retried. Achieve it by: **upsert/MERGE** on a key instead of blind INSERT; **delete-then-insert per partition** keyed on the run's logical date; and writing with deterministic output paths. Always ask 'what happens if this runs twice?'", "topic": "ETL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Full load vs incremental load? What is a watermark?", "a": "**Full load** reprocesses all data each run — simple but slow/costly at scale. **Incremental load** processes only new/changed rows since last run. A **watermark** is the marker of 'how far we've loaded' — usually the max `updated_at` or id — so the next run pulls `WHERE updated_at > watermark`. **CDC (Change Data Capture)** is an advanced incremental approach reading the DB change log for inserts/updates/deletes.", "topic": "ETL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Explain the medallion (Bronze/Silver/Gold) architecture.", "a": "A layered lakehouse pattern:\n- **Bronze**: raw ingested data, exactly as received (+ metadata). Auditable, reprocessable.\n- **Silver**: cleaned, deduped, typed, conformed, joined with reference data.\n- **Gold**: business-level aggregates/marts feeding dashboards, ML, and AI.\n\nEach layer is reproducible from the previous one, so you can rebuild downstream if logic changes.", "topic": "ETL", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "A nightly pipeline failed halfway. How do you recover safely?", "a": "1. **Make tasks idempotent** so a rerun is safe (upsert / overwrite-by-partition).\n2. Design around a **logical/run date** so reprocessing targets the right partition.\n3. The orchestrator (**Airflow**) **retries** the failed task; on success, downstream continues.\n4. If data was partially written, the partition overwrite replaces it cleanly — no duplicates.\n5. **Alert** on failure, log row counts, and run **data-quality checks** before publishing to Gold.\n\nKey: idempotency + partition-scoped writes turn failures into safe retries.", "topic": "ETL", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Why is a columnar format like Parquet better than CSV for analytics?", "a": "Analytics reads a few columns across many rows. **Columnar** storage groups each column together so the engine reads only requested columns (less I/O), compresses far better (similar values together), embeds types, and supports **predicate/column pushdown** (skipping chunks via min/max stats). CSV is row-based, untyped, uncompressed, and slow. A 1GB CSV often becomes ~100–200MB as Parquet with much faster queries.", "topic": "Warehouse", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Data lake vs data warehouse vs lakehouse?", "a": "- **Warehouse**: structured tables, schema-on-write, fast governed SQL (Snowflake/BigQuery/Redshift). Costlier, structured only.\n- **Lake**: any raw files in cheap object storage, schema-on-read. Flexible but can become a 'swamp'.\n- **Lakehouse**: a table layer (**Delta/Iceberg/Hudi**) over lake files adding ACID, schema enforcement, time travel — cheap *and* reliable. Best of both.", "topic": "Cloud", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is IAM and the principle of least privilege?", "a": "**IAM (Identity & Access Management)** controls *who* (principal: user/role/service) can perform *what* actions on *which* resources. **Least privilege** = grant only the minimum access needed for a task, nothing more — the top cloud security rule. E.g. a Spark job assumes a role that can read exactly one bucket, not the whole account.", "topic": "Cloud", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "How do you control cost in a cloud data platform?", "a": "- **Columnar + partitioning** so queries scan less (BigQuery/Athena bill by bytes scanned).\n- **Auto-terminate** idle clusters; right-size compute.\n- **Storage tiers** — move cold data to cheaper storage.\n- **Spot/preemptible VMs** for fault-tolerant batch.\n- **Incremental loads** instead of full reprocessing.\n- **Materialise** repeated heavy aggregations.\n- Monitor with cost dashboards/alerts.", "topic": "Cloud", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Design a data pipeline to ingest and analyse 1 billion events/day.", "a": "**Clarify first**: real-time or batch? latency SLA? query patterns? budget?\n\n**HLD**: events → **Kafka** (durable, partitioned ingestion) → land raw in **object storage** (Bronze, Parquet, partitioned by date/hour) → **Spark/Flink** transforms → **warehouse/lakehouse** (Silver/Gold star schema) → BI/ML. Orchestrate with **Airflow**; secure with **IAM**.\n\n**Scale math**: ~1B/day ≈ 11.5k events/s avg (plan for higher peaks) → size Kafka partitions and Spark parallelism accordingly.\n\n**LLD**: partition strategy, schema, idempotent upserts, compaction of small files.\n\n**-ilities**: retries/alerting, data-quality checks, exactly-once where needed, cost via partition pruning. State trade-offs (batch simplicity vs streaming latency).", "topic": "System Design", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "How would you design a RAG system over company documents?", "a": "**Indexing pipeline (offline ETL)**: ingest docs → clean → **chunk** (size + overlap, on semantic boundaries) → **embed** each chunk → store vectors + metadata (source, date, ACL) in a **vector DB**.\n\n**Serving**: embed the user question → retrieve top-k similar chunks (optionally **hybrid search** + **re-ranking**, with metadata/permission filters) → build a prompt instructing the LLM to answer **only from context** and **cite sources** → return answer + citations.\n\n**Quality/prod**: evals for retrieval + faithfulness, guardrails against prompt injection, caching for cost/latency, observability. Emphasise that quality is dominated by **chunking + data quality**.", "topic": "System Design", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "How do you approach any system design question?", "a": "1. **Clarify requirements** (volume, latency, consistency, budget) — never start drawing immediately.\n2. **Estimate scale** (rows/day, GB/TB, QPS) → choose batch vs streaming.\n3. **HLD** — components, data flow, tech choices *with reasons*.\n4. **LLD** — schemas, keys, partitioning, key logic, idempotency.\n5. **Address -ilities** — scalability, reliability, data quality, cost, security.\n6. **State trade-offs** — there's no perfect answer; show you understand the costs.", "topic": "System Design", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is RAG and why use it over fine-tuning?", "a": "**RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)** retrieves relevant info (via embeddings + vector search) and injects it into the prompt so the LLM answers from *your* data, grounded and current. Prefer RAG over fine-tuning when you need **fresh, factual knowledge**: it's cheaper, easy to update (just re-index), and reduces hallucination with citations. **Fine-tuning** is better for teaching new *behaviour/style/format*, not facts. Often you combine both.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["GenAI Engineer", "AI Data Engineer", "LLM Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is an embedding and a vector database?", "a": "An **embedding** is a vector (list of numbers) representing the *meaning* of text/image; similar meanings are close in vector space (measured by cosine similarity). A **vector database** (pgvector, FAISS, Pinecone, Chroma, Milvus) stores embeddings and finds the nearest ones to a query vector quickly via approximate nearest-neighbour search. Together they power semantic search and RAG retrieval.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["GenAI Engineer", "LLM Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Explain LoRA / QLoRA and full fine-tuning.", "a": "**Full fine-tuning** updates all model weights — expensive, full-size checkpoint per task. **LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation)** freezes the base model and trains tiny low-rank adapter matrices (~0.1–1% of params): far cheaper, small swappable adapters, ~95% of full quality for many tasks. **QLoRA** quantizes the base model (e.g. 4-bit) so LoRA training fits on a single GPU. Tools: Hugging Face transformers + PEFT.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["LLM Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "What causes hallucinations and how do you reduce them?", "a": "LLMs predict plausible text, not verified facts, so they can state falsehoods confidently. Reduce via: **RAG** to ground answers in real sources; instruct the model to answer **only from provided context** and say 'I don't know' otherwise; **lower temperature** for factual tasks; **citations** so users verify; **evals** measuring faithfulness; and **guardrails/validation** on outputs. You reduce, not eliminate — design for verification.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["GenAI Engineer", "LLM Engineer"]}, {"q": "What are tokens, context window, and temperature?", "a": "- **Token**: the unit an LLM processes — roughly ¾ of a word. You're billed per token and limits are in tokens.\n- **Context window**: max tokens the model can consider at once (its working memory) — bigger lets it read more at once.\n- **Temperature**: randomness knob; 0 = focused/deterministic (facts, code), higher = more creative/varied.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": [], "roles": ["GenAI Engineer", "LLM Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "How do you control chunking for good RAG quality?", "a": "Chunking dominates RAG quality. Guidelines: pick a **chunk size** (~200–800 tokens) balancing context vs precision; add **overlap** (~10–20%) so ideas aren't split; **split on semantic boundaries** (headings, paragraphs) not mid-sentence; attach **metadata** (source, section, date) for filtering and citations; and **evaluate** retrieval (are the right chunks returned?). Bad chunking is the #1 cause of poor RAG answers.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "Tell me about yourself.", "a": "Use a 60–90s structure: **(1) who you are now** (role + years/focus), **(2) 2–3 highlight projects with numbers/impact**, **(3) what you're great at / enjoy**, **(4) what you're looking for**. Lead with seniority signal, keep it specific (metrics > adjectives), and end forward-looking. Practise out loud until it's smooth, not memorised-robotic.", "topic": "Behavioral", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer", "LLM Engineer"]}, {"q": "Describe a challenging technical problem you solved.", "a": "Use **STAR**: **Situation** (context + why hard), **Task** (your responsibility), **Action** (what *you* specifically did — be technical and concrete), **Result** (measurable outcome). Pick a story showing ownership and a number (latency cut, cost saved, bug fixed). Anchor to *your* work ('I did X'), not the team's, and end with what you learned.", "topic": "Behavioral", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "How do you handle not knowing the answer in an interview?", "a": "Never bluff. Say: *'I haven't worked with that specific piece, but my understanding is X — and here's how I'd approach finding out.'* Then reason from fundamentals out loud. Interviewers value honesty + structured thinking over fake confidence. Bluffing that unravels under follow-ups is far worse than a calm 'I'd need to verify, but my mental model is…'.", "topic": "Behavioral", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer", "LLM Engineer"]}, {"q": "How does Spark handle data skew in joins, and what do you do if AQE is off?", "a": "**Skew** = one key has far more rows, so one task does most of the work and stragglers slow the whole job.\n\n- **AQE on (Spark 3.x):** `spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled` auto-detects oversized partitions and splits them at runtime.\n- **AQE off / manual:** **salt** the skewed key — add a random suffix (`key_0..N`) to spread it across tasks, replicate the small side per salt, then aggregate. Or **broadcast** the small side to avoid the shuffle entirely.\n\nConfirm skew in the Spark UI (one task far longer than the rest).", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What's the difference between cache() and persist() in Spark?", "a": "`cache()` is just `persist(MEMORY_AND_DISK)` — the default storage level for DataFrames. `persist(level)` lets you pick the **storage level**: `MEMORY_ONLY`, `MEMORY_AND_DISK`, `DISK_ONLY`, or `*_SER` (serialized — less memory, more CPU).\n\nOnly cache a dataset that's **reused** several times; it consumes memory and can evict other blocks. Call `unpersist()` when done.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Why are pandas (vectorized) UDFs preferred over regular PySpark UDFs?", "a": "A regular Python UDF serialises data **row by row** between the JVM and Python — slow, and it blocks Catalyst optimisation. **Pandas UDFs** (vectorized, Arrow-based) move data in **batches** as Arrow columns, so Python works on whole vectors at once — often 10–100× faster.\n\nBest of all: prefer **built-in Spark SQL functions** over any UDF; they run in the JVM/Photon with no Python round-trip.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is the small file problem and how does Databricks solve it?", "a": "Thousands of tiny files (common with streaming or frequent writes) make reads slow — huge metadata overhead and many tiny tasks.\n\nFixes:\n- **OPTIMIZE** compacts small files into larger (~1 GB) ones.\n- **Auto-Compaction** + **Optimized Writes** do it automatically on write.\n- **Liquid Clustering** (or legacy **Z-ORDER**) co-locates related data for skipping.\n\nThen **VACUUM** removes the now-unreferenced small files.", "topic": "Delta Lake", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "How do you delete or update individual rows in Delta without rewriting whole files?", "a": "Classic Delta uses **copy-on-write**: a `DELETE`/`UPDATE` rewrites every file containing a matched row — expensive for point changes.\n\n**Deletion vectors** (merge-on-read) instead mark deleted rows in a side file and skip them at read time — no file rewrite. Enable them on the table; a later **OPTIMIZE** physically purges them. Ideal for frequent small `DELETE`/`UPDATE`/`MERGE` (e.g. GDPR 'right to be forgotten' deletes).", "topic": "Delta Lake", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Z-ORDER vs Liquid Clustering — what's the difference?", "a": "Both improve **data skipping** by co-locating related values so queries read fewer files.\n\n- **Z-ORDER:** you run `OPTIMIZE ... ZORDER BY (cols)` — a manual, full re-clustering each time, with fixed columns.\n- **Liquid Clustering:** declare `CLUSTER BY (cols)` once; Delta clusters **incrementally** as data lands, and you can change keys without rewriting everything.\n\nLiquid Clustering is the modern replacement for both Z-ORDER and rigid partitioning.", "topic": "Delta Lake", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is Auto Loader and when would you use it?", "a": "**Auto Loader** (`cloudFiles`) incrementally ingests **new files** as they land in cloud storage, without rescanning the whole bucket. It tracks processed files (scalable listing / file notifications), **infers and evolves the schema**, and is exactly-once via checkpointing.\n\nUse it for continuous file landing (logs, exports, drops) feeding a Bronze table — it's the standard ingestion entry point in the medallion pattern.", "topic": "Delta Lake", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is Change Data Feed (CDF) and why is it useful?", "a": "**CDF** makes a Delta table emit the **row-level changes** (inserts/updates/deletes, tagged with `_change_type`) between versions. Enable `delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true`, then read `table_changes('t', startVersion)`.\n\nUse it to propagate changes downstream incrementally — e.g. update Silver→Gold or sync to another system — without recomputing the entire table.", "topic": "Delta Lake", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "How do you handle schema drift in an ADF + Databricks pipeline?", "a": "**Schema drift** = source columns change over time.\n- In **ADF** mapping data flows, turn on **'Allow schema drift'** and use `byName()` / column patterns so new columns flow through.\n- In **Databricks / Auto Loader**, set `cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode` (e.g. `addNewColumns`) with a `schemaLocation`; unexpected data lands in `_rescued_data`.\n- Always validate the **critical** columns explicitly so drift doesn't silently break downstream contracts.", "topic": "Cloud", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Explain checkpointing in Structured Streaming. What happens if the checkpoint is deleted?", "a": "The **checkpoint** stores stream progress — source **offsets**, aggregation **state**, and write-ahead logs — so a restarted query resumes exactly-once from where it stopped.\n\nIf you **delete the checkpoint**, the stream loses its position: it restarts as a brand-new query, typically **reprocessing from the start** (or skipping, per `startingOffsets`), and stateful aggregations reset. Never point two queries at one checkpoint, and treat it as critical state.", "topic": "Streaming", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "How do you implement SCD Type 2 in a streaming pipeline?", "a": "Use **`foreachBatch`** on the stream and run a Delta **MERGE** per micro-batch — expire the old current row, insert the new version:\n\n```python\ndef upsert(batch_df, _):\n    (tgt.alias('t')\n       .merge(batch_df.alias('s'), 't.key = s.key AND t.is_current = true')\n       .whenMatchedUpdate(condition='t.hash <> s.hash',\n                          set={'is_current':'false','end_ts':'s.ts'})\n       .whenNotMatchedInsertAll()\n       .execute())\n\nstream.writeStream.foreachBatch(upsert).option('checkpointLocation', path).start()\n```\n\nIt's idempotent because MERGE keys on the business key + `is_current`.", "topic": "Data Modeling", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "ADF: Copy Activity vs Mapping Data Flows — when is each cheaper?", "a": "**Copy Activity** just moves data (source → sink), billed cheaply per DIU-hour — use it for plain ingestion/landing with little or no transformation.\n\n**Mapping Data Flows** run on a **Spark cluster** (billed per vCore-hour, plus cluster start-up) — only worth it for real transforms (joins, aggregations, derivations), and pushing heavy logic into **Databricks/SQL** is often cheaper still. Rule of thumb: don't spin up Data Flows just to copy.", "topic": "Cloud", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "How do you manage data infrastructure as code?", "a": "Define resources (storage, clusters, workspaces, jobs, networking) declaratively so they're versioned, reviewable, and reproducible:\n- **Terraform** — cloud-agnostic, huge provider ecosystem (incl. a Databricks provider).\n- **Bicep / ARM** — Azure-native.\n- **Databricks Asset Bundles** — for jobs, notebooks, and pipelines.\n\nWire it into **CI/CD** so dev/stage/prod are identical and every change goes through a PR, not the console.", "topic": "Cloud", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "How do you protect PII with dynamic data masking in a lakehouse?", "a": "In **Unity Catalog**, attach a **column mask** (a UDF returning masked values unless the caller is privileged) plus **row filters** for row-level security; or use **dynamic views**: `CASE WHEN is_account_group_member('admins') THEN ssn ELSE '***' END`.\n\nLayer with column-level grants and tag PII columns so policies apply consistently. Data stays unmasked at rest; masking is enforced **at query time by identity**.", "topic": "Security", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer", "AI Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "How do you model a many-to-many relationship in a star schema without a cartesian explosion?", "a": "Don't join the two dimensions directly. Insert a **bridge (associative) table** holding the valid key pairs (e.g. account ↔ customer). The fact joins to the bridge, and the bridge joins to each dimension.\n\nTo stop measures double-counting across the many side, add an **allocation/weight factor** in the bridge (weights summing to 1) and multiply measures by it.", "topic": "Data Modeling", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What do broadcast joins do to worker memory, and when should you avoid them?", "a": "A **broadcast join** ships the **small** table to every executor so each joins locally — no shuffle, very fast. But that table is held **in each executor's memory** (and the driver collects it first).\n\nAvoid broadcasting when the 'small' side isn't actually small (more than a few hundred MB): you risk **driver OOM** on collect and executor memory pressure. Tune `spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold` (set `-1` to disable). AQE can also pick or cancel broadcasts at runtime.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is Photon and when does it help?", "a": "**Photon** is Databricks' **vectorized query engine**, rewritten in C++ — it speeds up SQL and DataFrame operations (scans, joins, aggregations) by processing columns in batches with SIMD. It's a drop-in (no code change) and shines on **SQL-heavy, large-scan** workloads.\n\nIt helps less for pure Python/UDF-bound or tiny jobs, and Photon clusters cost more per hour — so weigh the speed-up against the price.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "How do you monitor pipeline failures and set up alerting?", "a": "- **Job level:** Databricks Workflows / ADF have built-in retries + failure notifications (email / webhook / Teams).\n- **Metrics & logs:** ship to **Azure Monitor / Log Analytics** (or CloudWatch) and alert on failures, latency, and **data freshness**.\n- **Data quality:** assertions (Delta Live Tables **Expectations**, dbt tests) that fail loudly on bad rows.\n- **Observability:** track row counts, schema, and SLAs — page on missed freshness, not just crashes.", "topic": "Orchestration", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Vector search vs keyword search vs hybrid search — when do you use each?", "a": "**Vector (dense) search** matches *meaning* via embeddings + nearest-neighbour — great for paraphrases. **Keyword (sparse / BM25) search** matches *exact terms* — IDs, error codes, names, rare phrases. **Hybrid** runs both and fuses the scores, usually giving the best recall on real enterprise data.\n\nUse hybrid by default; pure vector misses exact-match queries like `SKU-12345`.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is reranking in RAG, and what's the trade-off?", "a": "After retrieval pulls the top-k candidates fast, a **reranker** (a stronger cross-encoder) re-scores each candidate against the query and reorders them — noticeably improving precision and answer quality.\n\n**Trade-off:** extra latency and cost. The usual pattern: retrieve many cheaply (e.g. 50), then rerank down to the best few (e.g. 5) for the prompt.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is the lost-in-the-middle problem?", "a": "LLMs attend best to the **start and end** of a long context and tend to **ignore evidence buried in the middle**. So stuffing more context can *hurt*.\n\nMitigate by retrieving fewer, higher-quality chunks (better top-k + reranking), compressing context, shrinking the window, and **placing the strongest evidence first or last**.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "When would you choose fine-tuning instead of RAG?", "a": "**RAG** for fresh or domain **knowledge** — just update the index, no retraining, cheap and current. **Fine-tuning** for **behaviour, style, or format**, a narrow repeated task, or to get a smaller/cheaper specialised model.\n\nThey combine well; try better prompting + RAG *before* fine-tuning, since fine-tuning is costlier and goes stale.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is an AI agent, and how is it different from a workflow?", "a": "An **agent** is an LLM plus **tools, memory, and control logic** that decides the next step at runtime and loops (decide → act → observe) until the goal is met. A **workflow** follows fixed, predefined steps.\n\nPrefer a **workflow** when the path is predictable — cheaper, faster, easier to debug. Use an **agent** only when the steps genuinely can't be known in advance.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is tool / function calling, and describe the loop.", "a": "**Tool calling** lets the model request an external function via structured **JSON arguments**; your app runs it and returns the result. The model only *asks* — your code executes.\n\nThe loop: **user asks → model selects a tool + arguments → app validates & executes → result returns → model answers, or calls another tool.**", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "How do you design a good tool schema for an agent?", "a": "Keep tools **narrow and single-purpose**, with **clear names**, **specific descriptions**, **typed** required inputs, and **small, structured outputs**. Good schemas make the model pick the right tool with valid arguments.\n\nAvoid giant catch-all tools and vague descriptions — they cause wrong or malformed calls.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "How do you handle tool-call failures in an agent?", "a": "**Validate** the model's arguments before executing; **retry** transient failures with backoff; return **clear error messages** the model can act on; **fall back** gracefully; **log traces** for every call; and require **human confirmation** for risky or irreversible actions (deletes, payments).", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "Sequential vs parallel tool calling — when do you use each?", "a": "Use **sequential** calls when step B depends on step A's output. Use **parallel** calls for **independent** lookups (e.g. fetch weather and stock price at once) to cut latency.\n\nMany modern models can emit several tool calls in a single turn — exploit that for independent work.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is memory in agents?", "a": "**Memory** is useful state carried across turns or tasks: conversation **summaries**, **user preferences**, **task progress**, retrieved **facts**, or persistent **external records** (a DB / vector store).\n\nShort-term memory fits in the context window; long-term memory is stored and retrieved on demand so the agent doesn't start from scratch each turn.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is a multi-agent system, and when should you avoid one?", "a": "Multiple **specialised agents** (e.g. researcher, planner, coder) collaborate under a **supervisor**, graph, or message protocol, each owning part of a task.\n\nUse it when roles are genuinely separable. **Avoid it when one agent is simpler** — more agents means more cost, latency, and failure modes.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is MCP (Model Context Protocol)?", "a": "**Model Context Protocol** is an open standard for connecting AI apps to external **tools and data sources** in a uniform way — a universal adapter, so you don't hand-wire every integration. Your app speaks MCP; each tool or data source exposes an MCP server. It's becoming the common way agents access the outside world.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "How do you evaluate an LLM app and an agent?", "a": "Define **task-specific success criteria** and a labelled dataset; measure outputs **automatically and with human review**; gate releases with **regression tests**.\n\nFor an **agent**, evaluate the whole **trajectory** — correct tool choice, argument accuracy, number of steps, cost, latency, and safe behaviour — not just the final answer.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": [], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is prompt injection, and how do you defend against it?", "a": "**Prompt injection** is malicious/untrusted text (in a web page, document, or user message) that tries to override your instructions, leak the system prompt, or misuse tools.\n\nDefence: **treat all external text as untrusted** — validate and isolate it, separate instructions from data, constrain tool permissions, and never let retrieved content silently change behaviour.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "hard", "companies": [], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is excessive agency in an LLM system?", "a": "**Excessive agency** = giving the model too much functionality, permission, or autonomy, so a mistake or attack can take harmful actions (delete data, send emails, spend money).\n\nFix with **least privilege**: scope tools tightly, require approval for risky actions, and constrain what the agent can reach. Least privilege, always.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is tracing / observability for agents, and why does it matter?", "a": "**Tracing** records the full run — prompts, model calls, **tool calls**, inputs/outputs, latency, tokens, cost, and decisions — so you can debug, replay, and explain failures.\n\nBecause agents are non-deterministic and multi-step, you can't fix what you can't see. Tools like **LangSmith** specialise in this.", "topic": "GenAI", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["AI Data Engineer", "GenAI Engineer"]}, {"q": "What is a DAG in Spark?", "a": "A **DAG** (Directed Acyclic Graph) is the plan Spark builds from your transformations — **directed** (data flows one way) and **acyclic** (no loops). Each node is a transformation step. It's built **lazily** and only executed when an **action** runs, which lets the Catalyst optimizer improve the plan before any data moves.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What's the difference between narrow and wide dependencies in Spark?", "a": "- **Narrow:** each parent partition feeds **one** child partition — no data movement. e.g. `map`, `filter`, `select`, `withColumn`.\n- **Wide:** a child partition needs data from **many** parent partitions, forcing a **shuffle** across the network. e.g. `groupBy`, `join`, `distinct`, `reduceByKey`.\n\nWide dependencies are where stages split and where performance is won or lost — minimise shuffles.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "What creates a new stage in a Spark job?", "a": "A **shuffle boundary** — i.e. a **wide dependency**. Spark pipelines narrow transformations together into one stage; the moment data must be shuffled (`groupBy`, `join`, `distinct`, `repartition`) it ends the current stage and starts a new one. Fewer shuffles → fewer stages → faster jobs.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "When does a Spark DAG actually execute?", "a": "Only when an **action** is called (`show`, `count`, `collect`, `write`, `save`). Transformations are **lazy** — they just extend the DAG. Each action triggers a **job**, which Spark splits into stages and tasks and runs. Handy follow-up: one action = one job.", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "easy", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}, {"q": "Explain the DAG → stages → tasks relationship in Spark.", "a": "**DAG** = the full graph of transformations. Spark splits it into **stages** at every shuffle boundary (wide dependency). Each stage then runs as a set of **tasks** — **one task per partition**, all in parallel across executors. So: DAG → stages (at shuffles) → tasks (per partition).", "topic": "Spark", "difficulty": "medium", "companies": [], "roles": ["Data Engineer"]}]);
